      "kids_on_bikes": "Kids on bikes",
      "kinetic_content": "A moving car stores energy — kinetic energy. KE = ½mv². Notice the v-squared again. At 30 mph a car has some kinetic energy. At 60 mph it has FOUR TIMES that. At 90 mph it has NINE TIMES. Every bit of that has to go somewhere in a crash — into crumple zones, airbags, and unfortunately, into people. This is the single biggest reason speed limits exist. Modern crumple zones are designed for ~35 mph impacts. Above that, the occupants start to see forces that human bodies cannot survive. Seatbelts, airbags, and crumple zones together cut fatality risk by ~75% at legal speeds — but they cannot fight physics forever.",
      "kinetic_energy_at_impact": "Kinetic energy at impact",
